SIEOS driver development kit

A driver is an ELF relocatable object, NAME.drv, that the kernel links when
it loads it.  Every global symbol of the kernel is its library: the headers
of /usr/include/ddk declare them (ddi.h: what a driver declares and the
services made for drivers; pci.h, mm.h, blkdev.h, net.h, display.h, hid.h,
poll.h, ...).  The kernel's own drivers are built the same way: /drv holds
them, and /boot/bootarch.tar (loaded by GRUB with the kernel) has a copy,
for the drivers the root device needs.

A driver declares its name, its phase and the devices it drives:

    #include "ddi.h"
    #include "pci.h"

    DDI_DRIVER("mydrv", DDI_PHASE_ROOT, "My card");
    DDI_ALIAS("pci1234,5678");         /* pciVVVV,DDDD (hexadecimal, no leading zeros) */
    DDI_ALIAS("pciclass,020000");      /* or pciclass,CCSSPP, pciclass,CCSS, platform,i8042 */

    int _init(void)                    /* once: find the devices (pci_find_all), register them */
    {
        ...
        return 0;
    }

At boot every device found is matched with the drivers' aliases (the most
specific name first); the driver of a device is loaded in its phase
(DDI_PHASE_DISPLAY with the console, DDI_PHASE_BOOT before the root is
mounted, DDI_PHASE_ROOT after) and its _init is called.

Build it (the kernel's code model: the driver is loaded in the kernel's
upper 2 GiB):

    gcc -c -O2 -ffreestanding -fno-pic -fno-pie -mcmodel=kernel -mno-red-zone \
        -mgeneral-regs-only -fno-stack-protector -fno-builtin -I/usr/include/ddk mydrv.c
    ld -r -o mydrv.drv mydrv.o

Load it now with modload mydrv.drv (as root), or copy it to /drv to have it
loaded at every boot when its device is there.  modinfo lists the drivers.

example/ is a driver that attaches to nothing and says hello: make, then
modload hello.drv.
